  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a>
    <main id="main-content">
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
        <h1 id="page-title">Cat FAQ</h1>
        <p>
          Quick answers about everyday cat behavior, care, and communication.
          Select any question to reveal the answer.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-list" aria-label="Frequently asked questions about cats">
        <div class="example">
          <button
            class="question"
            type="button"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="answer-1"
            id="question-1"
          >
            Why do cats purr?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-1" role="region" aria-labelledby="question-1" hidden>
            <p>
              Cats often purr when they feel content, but they can also purr to
              comfort themselves when stressed or recovering. The sound is part
              communication and part self-soothing behavior.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button
            class="question"
            type="button"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="answer-2"
            id="question-2"
          >
            Why do cats sleep so much?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-2" role="region" aria-labelledby="question-2" hidden>
            <p>
              Most cats sleep 12 to 16 hours a day because they are built for
              short bursts of energy. Frequent rest helps them conserve energy
              between play, exploration, and meals.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button
            class="question"
            type="button"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="answer-3"
            id="question-3"
          >
            Why does my cat knead blankets or laps?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-3" role="region" aria-labelledby="question-3" hidden>
            <p>
              Kneading usually starts in kittenhood and often signals comfort,
              trust, and relaxation. Many cats continue doing it as adults when
              they feel especially safe and cozy.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button
            class="question"
            type="button"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="answer-4"
            id="question-4"
          >
            What is the best way to keep an indoor cat entertained?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-4" role="region" aria-labelledby="question-4" hidden>
            <p>
              Offer a mix of climbing spots, scratching posts, puzzle feeders,
              and interactive toys. Rotating toys and scheduling short play
              sessions each day helps prevent boredom.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button
            class="question"
            type="button"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="answer-5"
            id="question-5"
          >
            Why do cats flick their tails?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-5" role="region" aria-labelledby="question-5" hidden>
            <p>
              Tail movement is a clue to mood. A gentle upright tail can show
              confidence and friendliness, while quick flicking may mean your
              cat is alert, overstimulated, or annoyed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <script>
      const questions = document.querySelectorAll(".question");

      questions.forEach((button) => {
        button.addEventListener("click", () => {
          const details = document.getElementById(button.getAttribute("aria-controls"));
          const isExpanded = button.getAttribute("aria-expanded") === "true";

          button.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(!isExpanded));
          details.hidden = isExpanded;
        });
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
